// 'include' is retired, and every way of writing it is one diagnostic.
//
// A module's files are its folder's files: a file joins a module by sitting in
// the folder whose file is named for it, and nothing in any file brings another
// in. module-folder-sweep is what does the job 'include' did.
//
// The word stays a keyword, so no program can bind it, and the statement it
// began is reported at the word and skipped whole. The last declaration shows
// recovery landing back on the global statement stream. None of the files named here
// exists, and none is looked for: had the compiler gone looking, a missing file
// would have aborted the compile at the first line instead.
//
// Parse stage, which is why this is its own file.

include helpers;                //~ ErrorInclude "the folder brings a module's files in"

// 'pub' speaks for a statement that is gone, so it adds no diagnostic of its own
pub include "sub/helpers";      //~ ErrorInclude "the folder brings a module's files in"

// A list of names, skipped to its ';' like the others
include webgl, mat4, shader;    //~ ErrorInclude "the folder brings a module's files in"

// The samples' form, a name per line with no ';'. The statement ends at its last
// name, so the declaration below is read as written. Scanning on for a ';' would
// have swallowed 'tail' up to the one inside its body, leaving its '}' to end
// the file early -- a second diagnostic, ErrorNoEof, which this count excludes
include nosemi                  //~ ErrorInclude "the folder brings a module's files in"
fn tail() i64 {
  2i64;
}
